  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather is expected through Thursday \nwith climbing temperatures.\n\nUpper level ridging slowly builds overhead beginning tonight \nwith surface high pressure remaining overhead through Thursday.\nAs of 9 PM, satellite imagery shows a stratocumulus deck that is\ngradually eroding. Winds are expected to go calm in most\nlocations tonight as high pressure builds overhead. With\ndewpoints generally in the upper 50s to low 60s, temperatures\nwon't have to fall far to reach saturation. Patchy fog is\nexpected to form overnight in locations that clear out and\nradiate, while locations that hang onto thicker cloud cover\nthrough the night likely won't have fog form. Some of the fog\ncould be locally dense, especially in deeper valleys to the west\nof the Blue Ridge. Any fog that does form will quickly dissipate\nafter sunrise tomorrow morning. \n\nWith upper level ridging and surface high pressure overhead, dry \nconditions and warming temperatures are expected through the end of \nthe week. High temperatures Wednesday will rise into the upper\n70s and lower 80s, with highs by Friday rising to near 90 for \nportions of the area. On Friday, surface high pressure shifts \noffshore as a cold front approaches from the northwest.\nDepending on the progression of this front, showers and\npotentially a thunderstorm or two could be possible by later\nFriday afternoon into Friday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stalling cold front and wave of low pressure \nmay affect the area this weekend.\n\nUpper troughing shears out toward the Great Lakes, eventually \nbecoming absorbed in a larger cyclonic gyre near Hudson Bay this \nweekend. At the surface, this pattern sends a cold front southward \ninto Virginia. There is some lingering uncertainty as to where the \nfront stalls, with a sharp temperature gradient across the frontal \nzone (60s on the north/cool side, 80s on the south/warm side). The \ntemperature forecast for Saturday may be subject to large changes of \n10 or more degrees as a result, with the current forecast splitting \nthe difference and calling for widespread 60s and 70s given at least \nsome broad consensus that the front ends up to the south placing the \nMid-Atlantic on its cooler and cloudier side. Shower activity may \naccompany the stalling front, with uncertainty in coverage (though \nanything severe or particularly heavy seems unlikely at this point).\n\nA wave of low pressure, infused with tropical moisture from the \nremnants of multiple East Pacific systems, treks toward the OH/TN \nValleys Saturday into Saturday night. The track of the low beyond \nthis point will depend on (1) where exactly the stalled front is, \nand (2) the strength of a transient blocking high pressure to the \nnorth. Rain or showers become increasingly likely for much of the \nregion Saturday night into Sunday, with a soaking rain possible if \nthe wave of low pressure is a bit more organized/closer to the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...High pressure likely returns early next week.\n\nFollowing the weekend system, a large area of high pressure will \nlikely build from the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes to the Northeast \nand Mid-Atlantic early next week. This sets the stage for much drier \nweather and below normal temperatures for the first half of next \nweek. Daytime highs on Monday and Tuesday likely stay in the 60s \n(50s in the mountains), with overnight lows in the 40s to near 50 \n(30s in the mountains and sheltered valleys west of the Blue Ridge \nwith perhaps some frost potential in the coldest spots).",
